Methodology: Detailed Description of the Systematic Approach for the Literature Review,
Including Data Sources, Search Strategies, and Selection Criteria.
The methodology of this review follows a systematic approach, adhering to established protocols for conducting literature reviews. The process is designed to ensure a comprehensive and unbiased collection, analysis, and synthesis of relevant literature on the use of big data in personalized marketing. This section details the data sources, search strategies, and selection criteria employed in this review.
Data Sources and Search Strategies:
The primary data sources for this review include academic databases, journals, and conference proceedings. Key databases such as PubMed, Scopus, Web of Science, and Google Scholar were systematically searched. The search strategy involved a combination of keywords and
phrases related to big data, personalized marketing, consumer behavior, and data analytics. The search was conducted using Boolean operators to combine these terms effectively and refine the search results (Cooper et al., 2018).
Selection Criteria:
The selection of literature for this review was guided by specific inclusion and exclusion criteria. Inclusion criteria encompassed peer-reviewed articles, published in English, from 2009 to 2023, focusing on big data applications in marketing, personalized marketing strategies, and consumer behavior analysis. Exclusion criteria included non-peer-reviewed articles, articles not
in English, and those not directly related to the core themes of big data and personalized marketing.
Literature Review Process:
The literature review process involved several stages, starting with the identification of relevant
articles through database searches. This was followed by screening titles and abstracts to assess their relevance to the review's objectives. Full-text articles were then retrieved and evaluated for inclusion based on the predefined criteria. The selected articles underwent a thematic analysis to identify and synthesize key findings, trends, and gaps in the existing literature
(Mertz, Strech, & Kahrass, 2017).
Quality Assessment:
To ensure the quality and reliability of the included studies, a quality assessment was conducted. This assessment evaluated the methodological rigor, validity, and relevance of each study to the review's objectives. The assessment criteria were adapted from established guidelines for
evaluating the quality of qualitative and quantitative research.


